<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  PRIVACY MANIFEST — App target.

  Apple has required one since May 2024 for any app whose binary calls a
  "required reason" API, and rejects the upload without it. This project had
  none: a search of ios/ for NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes returned nothing, while
  `@capacitor/preferences` uses UserDefaults (so did the widget bridge, until
  the extension was removed in Wave 5),
  which is category NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults. The failure is
  invisible until the first App Store Connect upload, which is the worst place
  to discover it.

  WHAT IS AND IS NOT DECLARED HERE:

  · NSPrivacyTracking is false and NSPrivacyTrackingDomains is empty. There is
    no analytics SDK, no ad SDK and no third-party network destination in this
    app — the only host it talks to is its own Netlify deploy and its own
    Supabase project. It never calls App Tracking Transparency because it has
    nothing to ask for.

  · The COLLECTED types are the ones this binary actually sends off device:
    health and fitness readings (HealthKit -> /api/ingest), and the account's
    email address (Supabase auth). Both are linked to the user — that is the
    point of an account — and neither is used for tracking or for anything but
    the app's own function.

  · Body measurements, sleep, workouts and nutrition all fall under
    NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealth / …Fitness. Apple's list has no finer
    grain and inventing one would be worse than using theirs.

  The native app and its widget bundle (native/) carry their own manifests.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealth</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeFitness</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  UserDefaults. Reached two ways: @capacitor/preferences persists the
			  app's own settings through it. CA92.1 is the correct reason — the app only
			  ever reads back data it wrote itself, in its own container.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
